  without saying anything to anyone.

  The [3mBluebell[0m had been the island ferry
  for sixty-one years. She was white
  and wooden and absurdly ornate, with a
  brass bell on her foredeck and a
  little upper saloon where the windows
  were etched with lilies. Walt had
  ridden her to school every weekday
  from the age of six. He had been
  seasick on her twice and in love on
  her once, on a crossing in a
  late-summer fog, when a girl named
  June Abernathy had shared her thermos
  of tea and asked him whether he
  thought the island was getting closer
  or the mainland was getting farther
  away.

  They had been married thirty-eight
  years. June was home now, with her
  knees up on a cushion and the radio
  on, and she did not know yet what the
  job was. Walt had not been able to
  make the words come out at breakfast.

  The new ferry was steel and fast and
  ran on batteries. It was a fine boat.
  Everyone said so. The [3mBluebell[0m had sat
  at Pier 9 for two years with a tarp
  over her wheelhouse while the town
  council argued about museums and
  grants and insurance, and in the end

